ngs  and they are a function of  of how we've had to manage to get  through this pandemic and i stand by  that and we want to do this few orders  as possible and yes we you know orders  closing businesses down are things that  i don't want to do and i absolutely  don't want to do  but we do  take to take them very seriously and  they they are orders  what i talked about last week was or  earlier this week was the least  restrictive means the things that we can  do that will um the minimum amount of  disruption to accomplish what we need to  do which is to minimize sickness and and  death to min to protect our health care  system and to minimize the disruption  that we're causing on society especially  the negative disruption so  this is uh  one of the things that we has worked for  us in the past and and consultation with  industry it makes it much cleaner if we  have an order and that it lays out all  of the the requirements under the order  so that's why we're doing it i  didn't mean to imply in any way that i  wouldn't do orders if they were  necessary it's mostly  we want to get through this part so that  we can come back to learning how to live  with this as another respiratory virus  in our lives and we're not there yet but  i do think we will get there and we'll  we'll get there as as you know as we get  through this this wave and hopefully  into the spring i think it's going to be  a very different situation for us and  and i won't need to to put in orders  anymore  um and i'm sorry i lost the second piece  of what you said  how how difficult is it to judge whether  the orders are working if  you know the data you're able to collect  on case count  yeah so case counts have only been one  thing that we've and and to be honest  we've been trying to move away from the  daily case counts as an indicator of  what's happening  because they only reflect uh case rates  and testing rates in different areas so  it's one of the surveillance markers  that we use and it'